  230: ## Dev Notes
  231: 
  232: ### Domain Placement — a NEW "Engine" entry, not a revival
  233: 
  234: The parent GitHub issue (#989) says "admin live roll feed panel (Engine domain)". **The Engine domain
  235: does not exist in the current app** — confirmed by grep, `public/admin.html` has no `data-domain`
  236: matching anything Engine-related, and gdx-5's own story (`specs/stories/gdx-5-game-in-progress-setting.md`,
  237: "Toggle Placement" Dev Note) already found and documented this exact staleness for a different
  238: story. `CLAUDE.md`'s own architecture section still lists "Engine (session log)" as a sidebar
  239: domain — equally stale, do not trust it.
  240: 
  241: Unlike gdx-5 (a single checkbox that fit naturally into an existing panel), this story's own live
  242: feed is a genuinely new, substantial admin surface with no natural existing home — Attendance
  243: (player-centric, payment-focused), ST Mods (unrelated concern), and every other existing domain were
  244: checked and don't fit. **This story adds a new sidebar button and domain section, using "Engine" as
  245: the id/label** — not because it revives the old (fully, deliberately removed in rlv.6/#836)
  246: dice-engine.js/feeding-engine.js/session-tracker.js tools, but because "Engine" is the most natural
  247: existing name for "live game-session tooling" and matches what a future ST would expect to find it
  248: under. This is a fresh addition, coincidentally sharing a name with something now fully gone.
  249: 
  250: **A related, separate, NOT-this-story's-job finding**: `public/js/admin/session-log.js`'s own
  251: `initSessionLog` export is imported in `admin.js` (`import { initSessionLog } from
  252: './admin/session-log.js';`) but never called anywhere — confirmed by grep. Its own file header says
  253: "Session log module — Engine domain in admin app" and its render target is `#engine-right`/
  254: `#engine-content`, neither of which exists in `admin.html` any more. This is a genuine, pre-existing
  255: dead-code gap (the rlv.6/#836 cleanup that removed its Engine-domain siblings missed this one import),
  256: unrelated to roll history — session-log is free-text session notes, not roll data. **Do not wire it
  257: up or delete it as part of this story.** Name it in Completion Notes so it's on record, and let
  258: Angelus decide separately whether it gets revived (now that an Engine domain exists again for a
  259: different reason), formally deleted, or left alone.
  260: 
  261: ### The exact hook point already exists — `addHist()`, not a new "what counts as a roll" decision
  262: 
  263: `public/js/suite/roll-v2.js`'s `addHist(pool, cls, lbl, cnt, verd)` (line ~1032) is called from
  264: exactly the three places a roll actually completes: the chance-die early-return (~919), the contested
  265: branch (~990), and the standard branch (~1020). This is the SAME "what counts as a completed roll"
  266: decision this story needs — do not re-derive it. Add the new POST call directly alongside each
  267: `addHist(...)` call, using the same local variables already in scope there (this file's own existing
  268: naming: `pool`/`eff+'d10'` for the pool string, `cls`/`lbl`/`cnt or wS or net` for
  269: class/label/successes, `cA`/`cB` for the raw per-die results array). `state.hist`/`addHist` itself is
  270: untouched — this story adds a parallel write, not a replacement.
  271: 
  272: ### Reuse GDX-7's already-computed spend, don't recompute it
  273: 
  274: `doRoll()` already computes `spend = _currentSpendDecision()` near its own top (gdx-7's own code,
  275: unmodified by this story) and already knows `spend.cost.vitaeCost`/`spend.cost.willpowerCost` — the
  276: exact real amounts about to be (or already) spent via `trackerAdj`. This story's `vitae_spent`/
  277: `wp_spent` fields read that SAME object, at the point in the function where the POST fires — do not
  278: build a second, independent cost calculation. If `game_in_progress` is off, GDX-7's own spend logic
  279: never fires either (byte-for-byte, per its own AC7), so `vitae_spent`/`wp_spent` are moot in that
  280: case anyway — this story's own AC8 already excludes the whole path.
  281: 
  282: ### `player_id` is server-derived, never client-trusted
  283: 
  284: `server/routes/downtime.js:613`'s own pattern — `player_id: String(req.user._id || req.user.id ||
  285: '')` — is the established, correct shape. The client never sends `player_id` in the POST body; the
  286: server sets it from the authenticated session. Mirrors this project's own general convention (also
  287: seen in `characters.js`, `contested-rolls.js`) of never trusting a client-supplied identity field
  288: that the server can derive itself.
  289: 
  290: ### The TTL-string gotcha is real and already bit this codebase once
  291: 
  292: `server/index.js:300-308`'s own comment on `contested_roll_requests`' TTL index is explicit: every
  293: writer on that collection stores `new Date().toISOString()` (a string), so MongoDB's TTL monitor
  294: never actually expires anything there, even though the index itself was created correctly. This
  295: story's own `rolled_at` field must be a genuine `Date` object at write time specifically to avoid
  296: repeating that exact, already-documented mistake in a brand new collection. Test this directly (AC9),
  297: don't just trust the route code — mirror `crd-1-contested-roll-request-shape.test.js`'s own approach
  298: of asserting the literal shape of what gets written, not just that an index exists.
