+# Adversarial review findings — gdx-8-roll-history
+
+## High
+
+- [Pass 1] None found.
+
+### [Pass 2] Every real roller branch generates a schema-invalid POST
+
+- **Severity**: High
+- **File:line**: `public/js/suite/roll-v2.js:932-938,1010-1016,1047-1053`; `public/js/shared/dice.js:8-25`; `server/schemas/roll_log.schema.js:25-27`
+- **Triggering input or sequence**: A player makes any roll while `game_in_progress` is true. All three call sites pass numeric `state.AGAIN` as `again_rule`, while the schema accepts only string/null. Standard and contested rolls additionally pass `wC`, an array of dice-chain objects shaped like `{ r: { v, s, x }, ch: [...] }`, while the schema requires each `results` item to be an integer from 1 through 10. Direct Ajv runs against the installed schema rejected both the real chain shape and a chance payload with `again_rule: 10`.
+- **Observable consequence**: The server returns 400 for every POST generated by `doRoll()`. `_logRoll()` swallows that rejection, so no player roll is persisted and no live WS event is emitted; the feature appears merely empty rather than visibly broken.
+- **Confidence**: Very high; confirmed by tracing the real dice return type and by runtime validation with the installed Ajv configuration.
+
+### [Pass 2] The ST-only roll feed is broadcast to every authenticated player
+
+- **Severity**: High
+- **File:line**: `server/ws.js:71-80,158-164`
+- **Triggering input or sequence**: Any player opens a WebSocket using their valid token and listens to raw frames while anybody successfully posts a roll. `broadcastRollLogged()` calls the unfiltered `_fanOut()`, which iterates every open socket; although each socket has `ws.user`, no role check is applied.
+- **Observable consequence**: A player who is correctly denied by `GET /api/roll_log` can nevertheless receive the full live ST feed, including every roller's `player_id`, character ID, dice, result, modifiers, and resource spend. The lack of a registered callback in the normal player UI is not an access boundary; browser code or devtools can read the socket directly.
+- **Confidence**: High.
+
+### [Pass 3a] Every real `doRoll()` payload fails the new server schema
+
+- **Severity**: High
+- **File:line**: `public/js/suite/roll-v2.js:932-937,1010-1015,1047-1052,1072-1083`; `public/js/shared/dice.js:8-28`; `server/schemas/roll_log.schema.js:25-27`; `server/tests/gdx-8-roll-history.test.js:215-280`
+- **Triggering input or sequence**: Any live-game roll reaches a new hook. `state.AGAIN` is numeric (`10`, `9`, or `8`), and `buildRollLogPayload()` preserves that number, while the schema permits only string/null `again_rule`; therefore even chance rolls are rejected. Standard and contested rolls also pass `wC`, whose items are `{ r: { v, s, x }, ch: [...] }` die-chain objects from `rollPool()`, while the schema requires each `results` item to be an integer.
+- **Observable consequence**: `POST /api/roll_log` returns 400 for every payload produced by the actual roller, so no completed roll is persisted or broadcast and the core feature is nonfunctional. `_logRoll()` absorbs the rejection. The new pure-function tests miss this by supplying hand-written string rules and flat integer arrays that production never supplies.
+- **Confidence**: High; executed both representative runtime shapes against the installed AJV configuration. A two-die `rollPool()` payload failed at `/results/0` and `/results/1`, and a chance payload with `again_rule: 10` failed at `/again_rule`.
+
+## Medium
+
+### [Pass 1] Live rolls can be duplicated or erased during an initial-feed fetch
+
+- **Severity**: Medium
+- **File:line**: `public/js/admin/roll-feed.js:29-51`
+- **Triggering input or sequence**: The ST opens or reopens the Engine domain, `initRollFeed()` starts `GET /api/roll_log`, and a `roll_log` WebSocket frame arrives before that GET resolves. `onRollLogged()` prepends the frame to `_feed`, but the eventual GET assignment replaces the entire array. Conversely, if the completed GET already contains a roll whose WS frame is processed just after it, the same roll is prepended again because there is no `_id` de-duplication.
+- **Observable consequence**: The ST's live feed can silently omit a roll until a later refresh, or show the same roll twice. Reopening the domain creates the same race again.
+- **Confidence**: High; both interleavings follow directly from the assignments in the diff.
+
+### [Pass 1] Any authenticated role with a matching `character_ids` entry can POST
+
+- **Severity**: Medium
+- **File:line**: `server/routes/roll-log.js:23-29`
+- **Triggering input or sequence**: An authenticated user whose role is neither `player`, `st`, nor `dev`, but whose session has `character_ids` containing the submitted string, POSTs a valid roll. The function only special-cases `st`/`dev`; it never requires `role === 'player'` before applying the ownership list.
+- **Observable consequence**: The implementation is broader than its claimed "player writes own character; ST/dev unconditional" boundary. Whether this is exploitable depends on which additional roles the application issues, which cannot be established from the diff-only pass.
+- **Confidence**: Medium; the code path is certain, but the existence and session shape of other roles requires repository context.
+
+### [Pass 1] The write schema admits semantically impossible and unbounded roll data
+
+- **Severity**: Medium
+- **File:line**: `server/schemas/roll_log.schema.js:19-31`
+- **Triggering input or sequence**: An authorized client sends `results: []`, `successes: -50`, successes inconsistent with the results, or extremely long non-empty `label`, `pool`, or `again_rule` strings. These satisfy the declared schema because `results` has no `minItems`, `successes` has no `minimum`, strings have no `maxLength`, and there is no cross-field consistency validation.
+- **Observable consequence**: Invalid records are persisted and broadcast into the ST feed; oversized strings can also inflate storage, WS frames, and feed rendering work up to the application's general request-size limit.
+- **Confidence**: High for acceptance by this schema; runtime validator configuration will be checked in Pass 2.
+
+### [Pass 1] A negative GET limit can evade the intended 200-entry cap
+
+- **Severity**: Medium
+- **File:line**: `server/routes/roll-log.js:53-56`
+- **Triggering input or sequence**: An authorized ST/dev requests `/api/roll_log?limit=-100000`. `Math.min(-100000, 200)` remains negative and is passed to MongoDB's cursor `limit()` rather than being clamped to a positive range.
+- **Observable consequence**: MongoDB negative-limit semantics can request a single batch whose absolute limit is much larger than 200, defeating the route's cap and potentially producing an expensive response as the collection grows.
+- **Confidence**: Medium-high; the arithmetic defect is certain, while the exact driver behavior will be verified against the installed version in Pass 2.
+
+### [Pass 2] Persisted `player_id` is the Discord account ID, not the authenticated player-record ID
+
+- **Severity**: Medium
+- **File:line**: `server/routes/roll-log.js:34-38`; `server/middleware/auth.js:64-72`; `server/tests/helpers/test-app.js:160-168`
+- **Triggering input or sequence**: In production, any authenticated user posts a valid roll. `requireAuth` supplies the Mongo player `_id` as `req.user.player_id` and the Discord account ID as `req.user.id`; it does not supply `req.user._id`. The route chooses `req.user._id || req.user.id || ''`, and the new test explicitly expects the Discord-shaped mock `id` rather than its distinct `player_id`.
+- **Observable consequence**: `roll_log.player_id` cannot be joined to the application's player documents by `_id`, unlike modern routes such as `history.js`, `cyoa.js`, and `ordeal-responses.js`. The stated `downtime.js` precedent does use the same Discord-ID pattern, but there it identifies the author of a nested recallable flag; it does not make the two authenticated identities interchangeable.
+- **Confidence**: High.
+
+### [Pass 2] A character switch during `doRoll()` can attribute an old roll to the new character
+
+- **Severity**: Medium
+- **File:line**: `public/js/suite/roll-v2.js:867-918,932-937,1010-1015,1047-1052`; `public/js/app.js:1290-1319`
+- **Triggering input or sequence**: The user starts a roll for character A when its tracker is not confirmed, so line 888 awaits a network load. Before it resumes, the user switches to character B through `pickChar()`, which synchronously replaces `state.rollChar` and resets pool state. `eff` was already captured from A before the await, but spend decisions and each `_logRoll(state.rollChar._id, ...)` read mutable state after it.
+- **Observable consequence**: The displayed dice result can be calculated from A's old effective pool but persisted and broadcast under B's character ID, with B/current modifier and spend metadata. The same mutable-state window exists across the awaited tracker adjustments.
+- **Confidence**: High.
+
+### [Pass 2] “Actually spent” fields can be recorded when the tracker deduction never persisted
+
+- **Severity**: Medium
+- **File:line**: `public/js/suite/roll-v2.js:905-912`; `public/js/game/tracker.js:78-88,273-307`
+- **Triggering input or sequence**: A live-game roll passes the affordability guard. `doRoll()` awaits `trackerAdj()`, then marks the cost as logged, but `trackerAdj()` only updates the optimistic local cache and calls `saveToApi()` without returning/awaiting its `fetch`. The tracker PUT then fails at the network or HTTP layer while the independent roll-log POST succeeds.
+- **Observable consequence**: The durable roll record says Vitae/Willpower was spent although the durable tracker remains unchanged. HTTP 4xx/5xx responses are especially invisible because `saveToApi()` does not inspect `res.ok`; network rejections are swallowed too.
+- **Confidence**: High.
+
+### [Pass 2] Chance-die records claim modifiers that the branch does not apply
+
+- **Severity**: Medium
+- **File:line**: `public/js/suite/roll-v2.js:918-937`
+- **Triggering input or sequence**: A roll's effective pool is zero or lower. The chance branch calls `d10()` once and only treats a natural 10 as success; it never calls the exploding/again or Rote paths. Nevertheless, it persists `again_rule: state.AGAIN` (normally `10`) and can persist `rote: true` if that toggle remains on.
+- **Observable consequence**: Persisted history and the ST feed describe the chance roll as “10-again” and/or “rote” even though neither mechanic affected its dice. Consumers cannot distinguish applied modifiers from stale UI selections.
+- **Confidence**: High that the fields disagree with branch execution; medium on desired presentation until the acceptance criteria are read.
+
+### [Pass 2] Contested failures with successes are painted as hits
+
+- **Severity**: Medium
+- **File:line**: `public/js/suite/roll-v2.js:961-1016`; `public/js/admin/roll-feed.js:73-92`
+- **Triggering input or sequence**: In a contested roll, the attacker gets one or more successes but draws or loses (for example attacker 3, defender 4). The record label becomes `Failure`, but `successes` is stored as the attacker's raw `wS` rather than zero/net; the feed chooses its hit border solely with `entry.successes > 0`.
+- **Observable consequence**: After the schema integration is repaired, the ST feed visually marks a failed or drawn contested roll as a successful hit, contradicting its displayed outcome label.
+- **Confidence**: High; this follows directly from the contested assignment and CSS-class predicate.
+
+### [Pass 2] Reconnect resumes callbacks but never fills the persisted-event gap
+
+- **Severity**: Medium
+- **File:line**: `public/js/data/ws.js:101-143`; `public/js/admin/roll-feed.js:28-52`
+- **Triggering input or sequence**: The ST leaves the Engine feed open, loses the WS connection while rolls occur, and reconnects automatically. The module-level callback remains registered exactly once, but `onopen` does not notify the feed or refetch `/api/roll_log`.
+- **Observable consequence**: Rolls persisted during the disconnect remain absent from the open feed indefinitely; they appear only if the ST switches away and reopens Engine (which reruns GET) or reloads.
+- **Confidence**: High; reconnect callback lifetime is correct, but there is no replay/resync path.
+
+### [Pass 3a] The disclosed spend-field deviation violates literal AC6 and still cannot prove durable spend
+
+- **Severity**: Medium
+- **File:line**: `specs/stories/gdx-8-roll-history.md:30-32,89-101,147-162,272-280`; `public/js/suite/roll-v2.js:890-912`
+- **Triggering input or sequence**: Any roll has a nonzero offered cost but fails the spend guard, or passes the guard while its background tracker PUT later fails. Literal AC6 requires the already-computed `spend.cost.vitaeCost`/`willpowerCost` values and the scope section says the story only reads the existing spend object/call sites. The implementation instead edits those call sites and introduces post-`trackerAdj` locals.
+- **Observable consequence**: This is an explicitly disclosed implementation deviation, not a hidden one, and recording zero when no deduction was attempted is semantically preferable to recording an offered cost. However, the stated justification overclaims “actually deducted”: `trackerAdj()` returns before its persistence request and silently ignores HTTP failure, so the new values prove only an optimistic local-cache decrement, not a durable deduction. The implementation therefore departs from the literal contract without fully achieving the rationale used to justify that departure.
+- **Confidence**: High.
+
+## Low
+
+### [Pass 1] Roll-log write failures are permanently invisible
+
+- **Severity**: Low
+- **File:line**: `public/js/suite/roll-v2.js:1061-1064`
+- **Triggering input or sequence**: Any POST fails because of schema drift, authorization/session drift, a route deployment mismatch, database failure, or a network error. `_logRoll()` attaches a completely empty rejection handler.
